equality between a sum and one term. Restore the intended power series followed
by its coefficient definition. Also disambiguate the trigonometric coefficients,
repair the mismatched partial-sum indices in the technology exercises, move
mathematical variables into math mode, and correct malformed hint punctuation.

A later pass found nine "expand about a center" exercises where the center
followed the function expression via a semicolon fused inside one math span
(e.g. "$f(x)=\frac{1}{1-x^{2}};a=0$"), unlike two already-correct items
earlier in this same list that join the function and center as two separate
$...$ spans with "; " between them ("$f(x)=\frac{1}{x}$; $a=1$." and
"$f(x)=\frac{1}{1-2x}$; $a=0$." -- that two-span joiner is a different,
unaffected construct). Split each of the nine into two comma-joined spans,
e.g. "$f(x)=\frac{1}{1-x^{2}}$, $a=0$", to match this file's established
single-span-parameter convention.

